    <div class="level-panel" id="panel-intermediate">
      <div class="card i18n" data-en="<p>The chapter's structure follows a deliberate logic: it starts by defining WHAT we're protecting (the CIA+ objectives), then HOW attacks are classified (the OSI architecture's attack/mechanism/service triad, refined into the passive/active split), then WHICH services counter those attacks (authentication, access control, confidentiality, integrity, nonrepudiation, availability — each with formal X.800 sub-definitions), then WHAT tools implement those services (the eight security mechanisms, and underneath them, the three cryptographic algorithm families), and finally WHY any of this works at all — the Trust Model, which grounds every technical guarantee in an underlying assumption about the behavior of other parties.</p><p>A key analytical skill: when given a scenario, first classify it as passive or active (does it alter data/resources, or only observe?), then map it onto the four active sub-types if active (masquerade, replay, modification, DoS) or the two passive sub-types if passive (release of contents, traffic analysis). Then ask which CIA+ objective is threatened, and which service/mechanism would be the appropriate countermeasure. This four-step mapping (attack type → sub-type → objective threatened → countermeasure) is exactly how exam scenario-questions are typically structured.</p>">
        <p>
